{:edb/db-before-t 1176 :edb/db-after-t 1177 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534489 50 #inst "2026-10-07T08:20:56.497Z" 13194139534489 true] [17592186096527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291614 13194139534489 true] [17592186096527 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291617 13194139534489 true] [17592186096529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291621 13194139534489 true] [17592186096530 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291626 13194139534489 true] [17592186096530 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291629 13194139534489 true] [17592186291614 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291615 13194139534489 true] [17592186291614 1089 "q-327713" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291614 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534489 true] [17592186291614 1100 "Given that the expression $2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291614 1113 #uuid "0b3e3683-194f-54fa-bd4c-fab6903bc098" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291614 1145 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291616 13194139534489 true] [17592186291615 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534489 true] [17592186291615 1041 "field-1" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291616 13194139534489 true] [17592186291615 1110 #uuid "8fe7058e-14c1-530a-b327-daaa9a9dd3a2" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291616 1034 "60" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534489 true] [17592186291616 1111 #uuid "0f0dc3cc-1fcc-5234-969d-d0c45c4086d2" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291617 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291618 13194139534489 true] [17592186291617 1089 "q-327714" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291617 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534489 true] [17592186291617 1100 "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291617 1113 #uuid "88777a8c-b856-53ff-a931-cd87ef035449" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291617 1145 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291619 13194139534489 true] [17592186291618 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291620 13194139534489 true] [17592186291618 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534489 true] [17592186291618 1041 "field-1" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291618 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291620 13194139534489 true] [17592186291618 1110 #uuid "95567c78-423d-51cf-921f-00d164d442d4" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291619 1034 "60" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534489 true] [17592186291619 1111 #uuid "3aa2e911-c5aa-57d2-ac62-d3b4d243113b" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291620 1034 "30" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534489 true] [17592186291620 1111 #uuid "f92fa46d-b9a4-5869-b341-90d7376697fc" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291622 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291624 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1089 "q-327990" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1100 "Express $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1113 #uuid "5112b0c9-2348-5c77-aec1-76fb30e4dd13" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291621 1145 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+27 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =36 \\\\ R & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+{60}^{∘})\n$$" 13194139534489 true] [17592186291622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291623 13194139534489 true] [17592186291622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534489 true] [17592186291622 1041 "field-1